WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.580
The first microwave sold for $5,000, the same physics later cooked dinner for under 100.

00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:09.180
This is First Principles Daily, episode 79.

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:16.200
In 1947, Raytheon put a radar magnetron in a refrigerator-sized cabinet and piped water

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:18.580
through that cabinet to keep the tube from melting.

00:00:19.220 --> 00:00:22.820
The machines stood taller than a person and weighed several hundred pounds.

00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:27.880
Restaurants were the only customers who could justify a commercial oven built that way.

00:00:28.580 --> 00:00:33.280
Dialectric heating of water at microwave frequencies was already cheap in principle.

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:37.840
The bill came from treating a kitchen heater as leftover wartime radar.

00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:41.600
The cavity magnetron itself was a wartime invention.

00:00:42.220 --> 00:00:49.060
John Randall and Harry Boote at the University of Birmingham built the first high-power cavity magnetron in 1940.

00:00:49.780 --> 00:00:53.080
And that tube became the heart of Allied centimetric radar.

00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:58.020
After the war, the firms that already made those tubes, Raytheon, above all,

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:02.360
looked at leftover magnetrons the way a foundry looks at leftover heat.

00:01:03.060 --> 00:01:08.120
Percy Spencer at Raytheon noticed a candy bar softening in his pocket near a live tube

00:01:08.120 --> 00:01:10.640
and the company did the analogical thing.

00:01:11.260 --> 00:01:17.200
Engineers aimed a radar transmitter at food and wrapped a metal box around the meal so the energy would not leak.

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:19.580
That is reasoning by analogy.

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:22.520
The product is a radar set that happens to cook.

00:01:23.140 --> 00:01:30.620
The 1947 radar range still needed a water connection because the magnetron was a high-power radar tube with a water jacket

00:01:31.240 --> 00:01:35.000
and the oven wanted far more power than a kitchen wall outlet likes to give.

00:01:35.740 --> 00:01:38.880
Through the 1950s, the home attempts stayed in that groove.

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:47.300
Tappen's 1955 domestic model was still priced around $1,300 and still asked for a 220-volt circuit

00:01:47.300 --> 00:01:50.920
because the industry had not dropped the military tube assumptions.

00:01:51.640 --> 00:01:59.320
Magnetrons were built in small lots to radar tolerances with expensive permanent magnets and hand-finished copper anode blocks.

00:01:59.980 --> 00:02:03.960
The cooking cavity was a fabricated metal cabinet, not a consumer shell.

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:10.020
Everyone accepted that a microwave oven was industrial equipment that might, someday, shrink a little.

00:02:10.540 --> 00:02:16.720
The finished cost sat in the thousands of dollars because the factory still thought of the vacuum tube as a weapon component.

00:02:17.380 --> 00:02:21.620
First principle starts by throwing out the radar set and keeping only the physics.

00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:28.880
Polar water molecules in food try to follow a rapidly reversing electric field and the lag shows up as heat.

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:35.880
You need a frequency where that loss is useful in a kitchen-sized portion, a metal box that confines the field,

00:02:36.320 --> 00:02:39.760
and a generator that can deliver something like a kilowatt of that field.

00:02:40.240 --> 00:02:45.560
2.45 GHz, already reserved as an industrial scientific medical band,

00:02:46.060 --> 00:02:51.780
gives a wavelength of about 12 centimeters and a penetration depth of a few centimeters in moist food,

00:02:52.240 --> 00:02:54.400
enough to heat a plate, not a side of beef.

00:02:55.060 --> 00:02:57.220
That is the entire physical requirement.

00:02:57.940 --> 00:02:59.520
Now the magic wand number.

00:03:00.100 --> 00:03:05.020
If the atoms of a household oven could be arranged for free, what would those atoms cost?

00:03:05.820 --> 00:03:12.400
The cavity and chassis are thin mild steel because a fair-day cage at this wavelength does not need armor plate.

00:03:12.980 --> 00:03:17.740
A rough guess for a compact unit is several kilograms of sheet, not a hundred weight.

00:03:18.380 --> 00:03:23.320
Price that steel at even a full dollar a kilogram and the box is still a handful of dollars.

00:03:24.040 --> 00:03:26.760
The magnetron adds a small copper anode block,

00:03:27.180 --> 00:03:30.660
centered ferrite magnets, ceramic insulators, and a cathode.

00:03:31.180 --> 00:03:33.440
Commodity masses, not a treasure chest.

00:03:34.020 --> 00:03:38.400
The high voltage transformer is the other lump of copper wire and iron laminations,

00:03:38.900 --> 00:03:40.920
heavier than the tube and still just metal.

00:03:41.740 --> 00:03:45.120
Add a glass tray, some plastic, a door screen, and a fan,

00:03:45.580 --> 00:03:48.780
and a back of the envelope materials pile lands around $10,

00:03:49.340 --> 00:03:51.920
or the mid teens if you are generous with the copper.

00:03:52.480 --> 00:03:54.460
That pile is the theoretical slower.

00:03:55.100 --> 00:03:59.020
Set the floor against a $1947 price of about $5,000.

00:03:59.600 --> 00:04:04.860
Even if you grant that first water-cooled cabinet $20 or $30 of metal and magnet,

00:04:05.160 --> 00:04:09.820
which is generous, $5,000 divided by $30 is still more than $160,

00:04:10.460 --> 00:04:13.680
and $5,000 divided by $20 is $250.

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:16.580
The idiot index sits in the low hundreds.

00:04:17.180 --> 00:04:18.520
The atoms were not the bill.

00:04:18.520 --> 00:04:22.060
The first redesign that mattered was the magnetron itself.

00:04:22.700 --> 00:04:25.620
Drop the water jacket and cool the anode with a fan,

00:04:26.040 --> 00:04:29.220
because a kitchen tube running at several hundred watts of output

00:04:29.220 --> 00:04:32.960
does not have a radar transmitter's duty cycle or power density.

00:04:33.560 --> 00:04:37.660
Losing the plumber's hookup turned the machine from a built-in into a countertop

00:04:37.660 --> 00:04:39.620
that could use an ordinary outlet.

00:04:40.120 --> 00:04:42.940
That cost-cut never appears on a bill of materials,

00:04:43.420 --> 00:04:46.200
and yet the cut decides whether a household will buy one.

00:04:46.760 --> 00:04:48.340
The magnets changed next.

00:04:48.920 --> 00:04:51.760
Fairright, pressed and centered like any other ceramic,

00:04:52.240 --> 00:04:56.100
took the place of the expensive alloy magnets that radar tubes had used,

00:04:56.460 --> 00:05:00.620
because an oven magnetron needs a magnetic field across the interaction space,

00:05:01.040 --> 00:05:03.220
not a military specification for drift.

00:05:03.880 --> 00:05:09.200
Japanese tube makers, among them Toshiba, Hitachi, Panasonic, and New Japan radio,

00:05:09.700 --> 00:05:14.080
then treated the copper anode and the vacuum process as a consumer component.

00:05:14.800 --> 00:05:20.340
Cavities sized for 2.45 gigahertz were brazed and exhausted on automated lines,

00:05:20.760 --> 00:05:22.840
rather than hand-finished to radar drawings.

00:05:23.540 --> 00:05:25.840
The resonant cavities have to be the right size.

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:29.000
Those cavities do not have to be laboratory jewelry,

00:05:29.500 --> 00:05:31.720
and once process control replaces craft,

00:05:32.320 --> 00:05:35.720
the tube's idiot index collapses toward its copper and ferrite.

00:05:36.340 --> 00:05:40.080
Around the tube, the cooking cavity stopped being a fabricated cabinet

00:05:40.080 --> 00:05:43.020
and became a stamped and spot-welded steel box,

00:05:43.020 --> 00:05:48.120
because the only electromagnetic job that box has is to be a conductor with holes

00:05:48.120 --> 00:05:49.540
much smaller than a wavelength.

00:05:50.220 --> 00:05:53.020
Skin depth and steel at this frequency is tiny,

00:05:53.480 --> 00:05:57.420
so thickness is set by stiffness and rust, not by radio engineering,

00:05:57.800 --> 00:06:00.000
and a press can make that shape in a stroke.

00:06:00.760 --> 00:06:05.980
The waveguide that launches energy from the magnetron antenna into the box followed the same logic,

00:06:06.480 --> 00:06:09.820
a formed sheet metal channel instead of machined radio plumbing.

00:06:10.380 --> 00:06:15.420
Door leakage, the obvious objection, was solved with a quarter-wave choke around the frame,

00:06:15.780 --> 00:06:17.320
rather than a massive gasket.

00:06:17.900 --> 00:06:23.520
A clever bit of geometry lets a cheap stamped door meet safety limits without precision machining.

00:06:24.180 --> 00:06:28.500
Standing waves inside a cheap rectangular box still leave cold spots,

00:06:28.840 --> 00:06:32.520
so sharps turntable, introduced in the mid-1960s,

00:06:32.900 --> 00:06:36.840
moved the food through the pattern instead of demanding a perfectly stirred mode.

00:06:37.480 --> 00:06:41.280
That turntable is another case of refusing to overbuild the cavity.

00:06:41.980 --> 00:06:46.760
Amana's 1967 countertop radar range, sold for about $500,

00:06:47.480 --> 00:06:49.940
proved the form factor in the United States,

00:06:50.360 --> 00:06:55.280
and the Japanese industry spent the next decade putting stamped cavities and volume magnetrons

00:06:55.280 --> 00:06:56.280
under that shape.

00:06:57.080 --> 00:07:00.200
Each of those moves attacks a different slice of the idiot index,

00:07:00.920 --> 00:07:03.100
cooling an installation, magnet cost,

00:07:03.100 --> 00:07:06.400
tube labor, cavity labor, radio plumbing,

00:07:06.720 --> 00:07:08.900
door hardware, and field uniformity.

00:07:09.440 --> 00:07:13.920
Each move is a direct consequence of naming the physics instead of copying the radar.

00:07:14.560 --> 00:07:19.320
By the late 1970s, a basic household microwave could be had for under $100,

00:07:19.920 --> 00:07:25.140
and through the 1980s the machine stopped being a novelty and became ordinary kitchen equipment.

00:07:25.740 --> 00:07:30.300
The magnetron had completed the journey from a radar spare that cost hundreds of dollars

00:07:30.300 --> 00:07:34.800
to a volume component whose price was a small fraction of that early tube.

00:07:35.360 --> 00:07:39.780
Take that $100 oven against the rough $10 to $15 materials pile.

00:07:40.480 --> 00:07:45.380
A hundred divided by 15 is about 7. A hundred divided by 10 is 10.

00:07:45.980 --> 00:07:48.340
The idiot index is no longer in the hundreds.

00:07:48.900 --> 00:07:52.140
A listener who does the division get something like high single digits,

00:07:52.680 --> 00:07:55.960
maybe 10, depending on how much copper goes into the transformer.

00:07:56.640 --> 00:07:59.880
That ratio is close enough to the floor that retail margin

00:07:59.880 --> 00:08:04.920
freight, safety certification, and a few screws now matter as much as metal.

00:08:05.460 --> 00:08:11.260
Assembly labor, the bulky transformer, door interlocks, and the simple fact that the unit ships

00:08:11.260 --> 00:08:17.440
in a box through a retail chain still sit on top of the atoms, and cheap ovens are generally not

00:08:17.440 --> 00:08:24.140
designed to be repaired. The physics extracted its own trade-offs. Standing waves still heat unevenly,

00:08:24.460 --> 00:08:28.400
and because the energy couples to water rather than to a hot dry surface,

00:08:28.400 --> 00:08:34.100
a microwave will not brown or roast unless you add a separate crisp plate or a convection element.

00:08:34.880 --> 00:08:38.520
The magnetron remains a vacuum tube with a cathode that wears out,

00:08:38.940 --> 00:08:44.060
which is why a 20-year-old oven often dies by going dark rather than by rusting through.

00:08:44.820 --> 00:08:50.040
Solid state radio frequency transistors can already generate cooking power in some premium and

00:08:50.040 --> 00:08:55.360
industrial machines, but those transistors have not yet been driven down the same volume curve

00:08:55.360 --> 00:09:02.800
of the cavity magnetron road in the 1970s. The cheapest kilowatt of 2.45 gigahertz in a stamped box

00:09:02.800 --> 00:09:08.740
is still that air-cooled tube. The hard, unsolved piece is not confinement or stamping.

00:09:09.380 --> 00:09:14.720
Those jobs are done. It is replacing the last vacuum tube in the kitchen without climbing back up

00:09:14.720 --> 00:09:20.280
the idiot index. The water jacket on the first radar range was a confession that the product had

00:09:20.280 --> 00:09:26.140
inherited a radar's duty cycle instead of a casseroles. Inherit the field and the frequency,

00:09:26.660 --> 00:09:31.760
leave the parent machines plumbing and tolerances behind. That is the first portable move,

00:09:32.240 --> 00:09:37.560
and the move applies anywhere a technology is slumming in a new job still dressed for the old one.

00:09:38.160 --> 00:09:43.140
A foundry does not pour a new alloy and then keep the old furnace's chimney because the chimney

00:09:43.140 --> 00:09:48.940
is familiar. The second move is that a fair-day cage at 12 centimeters is a stamping problem,

00:09:48.940 --> 00:09:55.100
not a cabinet-making problem, and the cost of the box will not fall until someone says so out loud

00:09:55.100 --> 00:10:01.180
and puts the box on a press. Armor plate and hand-finished copper made sense when the parent machine

00:10:01.180 --> 00:10:07.160
was hunting aircraft. Those tolerances make no sense when the job is to heat leftovers on a Tuesday.

00:10:07.980 --> 00:10:14.140
Japan did not invent dielectric heating. Japanese plants invented the magnetron as a consumer part

00:10:14.140 --> 00:10:18.940
and the cavity as a stamped commodity, and the price followed those two decisions down.

00:10:19.560 --> 00:10:24.560
The field stayed the same, the frequency stayed the same, what changed was who was allowed

00:10:24.560 --> 00:10:31.020
to treat a vacuum tube like a light bulb and a metal box like a baking pan. Tomorrow another example

00:10:31.020 --> 00:10:36.340
or another opening. The live question is who will do to solid-state cooking what the Japanese

00:10:36.340 --> 00:10:41.820
two plants did to the cavity magnetron and whether the first signal will be a transistor price

00:10:41.820 --> 00:10:47.680
or another stamped box that no longer needs a tube at all. That's a wrap on today's first

00:10:47.680 --> 00:10:53.360
principles breakdown. The ideas travel further when you pass them on. Send this to a fellow builder.

